# Project: HTL (HTML Template Language) Development Standards

## HTL Overview

HTL (HTML Template Language), formerly known as Sightly, is Adobe's server-side template system for HTML. It separates logic from markup to improve security and maintainability.

**Specification Version:** 1.4 (Final release, 18 June 2018)  
**Full Specification:** See `.cursor/rules/htl-specification.md` for complete HTL specification reference  
**Online Reference:** https://github.com/adobe/htl-spec/blob/master/SPECIFICATION.md

## Core HTL Principles

### 1. Security First
- **Context-aware output**: Always use appropriate context attributes (`@ context="html"`, `@ context="unsafe"`, etc.)
- **No inline JavaScript**: Never embed JavaScript directly in HTL templates
- **Expression escaping**: HTL automatically escapes output based on context

### 2. Separation of Concerns
- **Logic in Java**: Business logic belongs in Sling Models, not templates
- **Templates for presentation**: HTL templates should only handle rendering
- **Use Sling Models**: Access data through `data-sly-use` with Sling Models

### 3. Expression Syntax
- **EL expressions**: Use `${expression}` for output
- **Attribute expressions**: Use `${expression}` in HTML attributes
- **Block expressions**: Use `data-sly-*` attributes for control flow
- **Expression grammar**: `expression = '${' , [exprNode] , [ , '@' , optionList] , '}'`
- **Escaping**: Prefix `${` with backslash (`\${`) to escape expressions in output

## HTL Block Statements

### data-sly-use (Variable Declaration / Use-API)
```html
<!-- Load Sling Model (Java Use-API) -->
<sly data-sly-use.model="ai.typerefinery.websight.models.components.MyModel">

<!-- Load template file -->
<sly data-sly-use.template="./template.html">

<!-- Load with parameters -->
<sly data-sly-use.item="${resource.adaptTo(MyModel.class)}">
<div data-sly-use.nav="${'Navigation' @ depth=1, showVisible=!wcmmode.edit}">${nav.foo}</div>
```

**Specification Details:**
- **Element:** always shown (attribute removed from output)
- **Attribute value:** required; evaluates to `String`; the object to instantiate
- **Attribute identifier:** optional; custom identifier name; if omitted, uses `useBean` identifier
- **Scope:** Identifier is global to the script, available anywhere after declaration
- **Parameters:** Pass via expression options: `@ param1=value1, param2=value2`

**Use-API Object Resolution:**
1. Try to resolve as publicly accessible field
2. Try to resolve as method without parameters:
   - Method named `identifier`
   - Getter `getIdentifier` (camelCase)
   - Boolean getter `isIdentifier` (camelCase)
3. Return `null` if not found

**Rules:**
- Always use fully qualified class names for Sling Models
- Use relative paths for template includes (e.g., `./template.html`)
- Store loaded objects in descriptive variable names (`model`, `template`, `item`)
- Top-level identifiers are case-insensitive; properties are case-sensitive
- Java Use-API: POJO with default constructor or `init(Bindings bindings)` method
- JavaScript Use-API is deprecated in AEM Cloud Service - use Java Use-API

### data-sly-test (Conditional Rendering)
```html
<!-- Simple condition -->
<sly data-sly-test="${model.hasContent}">
  <div>Content exists</div>
</sly>

<!-- Store result in variable -->
<sly data-sly-test.hasContent="${model.items && model.items.size > 0}">
  <sly data-sly-test="${hasContent}">
    <!-- Use stored variable -->
  </sly>
</sly>

<!-- Negation -->
<sly data-sly-test="${!model.isEmpty}">
  <!-- Render when NOT empty -->
</sly>
```

**Rules:**
- Use `data-sly-test` to conditionally render blocks
- Store boolean results in variables for reuse: `data-sly-test.varName="${expression}"`
- Prefer explicit conditions over implicit truthiness
- Use `!` operator for negation, not `== false`

### data-sly-list (Iteration)
```html
<!-- Basic iteration -->
<sly data-sly-list.item="${model.items}">
  <div>${item.name}</div>
</sly>

<!-- With index and status -->
<sly data-sly-list.item="${model.items}">
  <sly data-sly-test="${itemList.first}">
    <!-- First item -->
  </sly>
  <sly data-sly-test="${itemList.last}">
    <!-- Last item -->
  </sly>
  <sly data-sly-test="${!itemList.last}">,</sly>
</sly>

<!-- Iterate with begin/end -->
<sly data-sly-list="${model.items @ begin=0, end=5}">
  <!-- First 6 items (begin inclusive, end inclusive) -->
</sly>
```

**Specification Details:**
- **Element:** shown for each iteration
- **Attribute value:** required; iterable object
- **Attribute identifier:** optional; iteration variable name; creates `itemList` status object
- **Status object properties:** `itemList.first`, `itemList.last`, `itemList.index`, `itemList.count`
- **Options:** `@ begin=number, end=number` for slicing (both inclusive)

**Rules:**
- Always specify iteration variable name: `data-sly-list.itemName`
- Use `itemList.first`, `itemList.last`, `itemList.index`, `itemList.count` for iteration status
- Use `itemList.last` check for comma-separated lists in JSON
- Use `@ begin` and `@ end` for pagination/slicing (both indices are inclusive)
- Element is rendered for each iteration

### data-sly-repeat (Alternative Iteration)
```html
<!-- Similar to data-sly-list but element shown only once, content repeated -->
<div data-sly-repeat.item="${model.items}">
  ${item.name}
</div>
```

**Rules:**
- Use when you want the element wrapper shown once, content repeated
- Less commonly used than `data-sly-list`

### data-sly-call (Template Call)
```html
<!-- Call template with parameters -->
<sly data-sly-call="${template.myTemplate @ param1=value1, param2=value2}">

<!-- Call with current context -->
<sly data-sly-call="${template.item @ item=item}">
```

**Rules:**
- Define templates with `data-sly-template` before calling
- Pass parameters explicitly using `@ param=value` syntax
- Use descriptive template names matching their purpose

### data-sly-template (Template Definition)
```html
<template data-sly-template.item="${ @ item, model}">
  <div class="${item.className}">
    ${item.content @ context="html"}
  </div>
</template>
```

**Rules:**
- Define templates at the top of files or in separate template files
- Use `@` to indicate parameter list
- List all parameters explicitly: `${ @ param1, param2}`
- Use descriptive template names

### data-sly-resource (Resource Inclusion)
```html
<!-- Include child resource -->
<sly data-sly-resource="${child.path}"></sly>

<!-- Include with specific resource type -->
<sly data-sly-resource="${child.path @ resourceType='my/component'}"></sly>

<!-- Include with decoration -->
<sly data-sly-resource="${child.path @ decorationTagName='section'}"></sly>
```

**Rules:**
- Use for including child components/resources
- Specify `resourceType` when overriding component type
- Use `decorationTagName` for semantic HTML wrapping

### data-sly-set (Variable Assignment)
```html
<!-- Set simple variable -->
<sly data-sly-set.title="${model.title || 'Default Title'}">

<!-- Set with expression -->
<sly data-sly-set.fullName="${[model.firstName, model.lastName] @ join=' '}">

<!-- Use variable -->
<div>${title}</div>
```

**Rules:**
- Use for complex expressions that are reused multiple times
- Prefer descriptive variable names
- Use `@ join` for array concatenation

### data-sly-include (File Include)
```html
<!-- Include another HTL file -->
<sly data-sly-include="./partial.html"></sly>

<!-- Include with context -->
<sly data-sly-include="${template.path}"></sly>
```

**Rules:**
- Use relative paths for includes
- Prefer `data-sly-use` + `data-sly-call` over `data-sly-include` for templates
- Use `data-sly-include` for static file includes

### data-sly-element (Dynamic Element)
```html
<!-- Render different HTML elements -->
<div data-sly-element="${model.tagName}">
  Content
</div>
```

**Rules:**
- Use when element type is determined at runtime
- Ensure model provides valid HTML element names
- Validate element names to prevent XSS

### data-sly-text (Text Content)
```html
<!-- Replace element content -->
<p data-sly-text="${properties.jcr:title}">This text would never be shown.</p>
<p data-sly-text="${'<strong>Bold</strong>' @ context='html'}"></p>
```

**Specification Details:**
- **Element:** always shown
- **Content:** replaced with evaluated result
- **Attribute value:** required; evaluates to `String`
- **Auto XSS protection:** uses `text` context unless overridden
- **Falsy values:** Cast to strings (empty string, empty array output empty; `0` outputs "0", `false` outputs "false")

**Rules:**
- Use to annotate designer HTML without modifying mock content
- Content is automatically XSS-protected with `text` context

### data-sly-attribute (Dynamic Attributes)
```html
<!-- Single attribute -->
<div class="className" data-sly-attribute.class="${myVar}">  <!-- Overwrites class -->

<!-- Multiple attributes from Map -->
<div data-sly-attribute="${model.attributes}">
<!-- Assuming model.attributes = {'id': 'foo', 'class': 'bar'} -->
<!-- outputs: <div id="foo" class="bar"> -->
```

**Specification Details:**
- **Element:** always shown
- **Content:** always shown
- **Attribute value:** optional; `String` for content, `Boolean` for boolean attributes, `Object` (Map) for multiple attributes
- **Attribute identifier:** optional; attribute name; must be omitted if value is `Object`
- **Processing order:** Left-to-right (later attributes override earlier)
- **Empty values:** Empty string removes the attribute
- **XSS protection:** Attribute name and content automatically protected
- **Restrictions:** Cannot generate `on*` event handlers or `style` attribute (no safe context)

**Rules:**
- Use for conditional or dynamic attributes
- Can accept Map of attributes for multiple at once
- Empty string values remove attributes
- Attributes processed left-to-right
- Cannot use for event handlers (`onclick`, etc.) or `style` attribute

## Expression Language Details

### Operators

#### Logical Operators (Precedence Order)
```html
${varOne && !(varTwo || varThree)}  <!-- 1. Grouping parenthesis -->
${!myVar}                            <!-- 2. Logical NOT -->
${varOne && varTwo}                  <!-- 3. Logical AND -->
${varOne || varTwo}                  <!-- 4. Logical OR -->
${varChoice ? varOne : varTwo}       <!-- 5. Conditional (ternary) - ':' must have spaces -->
```

**Rules:**
- `&&` and `||` work like JavaScript: return operand value, not just boolean
- Use `||` for default values: `${properties.title || 'Default'}`
- Ternary operator `:` separator must be surrounded by spaces

#### Comparison Operators (Strict, No Type Conversion)
```html
${stringValueOne == stringValueTwo}    <!-- String comparison (like JS ===) -->
${stringValueOne != stringValueTwo}    <!-- String comparison (like JS !==) -->
${numberValueOne < numberValueTwo}     <!-- Number comparison -->
${numberValueOne <= numberValueTwo}    <!-- Number comparison -->
${numberValueOne == numberValueTwo}    <!-- Number comparison -->
${numberValueOne >= numberValueTwo}    <!-- Number comparison -->
${numberValueOne > numberValueTwo}     <!-- Number comparison -->
${booleanValueOne == booleanValueTwo}  <!-- Boolean comparison -->
${nullValueOne == nullValueTwo}        <!-- Null comparison -->
${enumConstant == 'CONSTANT_NAME'}     <!-- Java Enum comparison -->
```

**Rules:**
- `==` and `!=` are strict (like JavaScript `===` and `!==`)
- No automatic type conversion between operands
- Use same type for both operands

#### Relational Operator: `in`
```html
<!-- Check if string contains substring (case-sensitive) -->
${'a' in 'abc'}        <!-- returns true -->
${'ab' in 'abc'}       <!-- returns true -->
${'d' in 'abc'}        <!-- returns false -->

<!-- Check if array/list contains value -->
${100 in myArray}      <!-- returns true if 100 in array -->

<!-- Check if object/Map has property/key -->
${'a' in logic}        <!-- returns true if object has property 'a' -->
```

### Type Casting

#### Boolean Casting
These evaluate to **false**:
- `false`
- `0` (zero)
- `''` or `""` (empty string)
- `[]` (empty iterable)

These evaluate to **true**:
- `"false"` (non-empty string, even if content is "false")
- `[0]` (non-empty iterable)

#### String Casting
```html
${0}              <!-- outputs: 0 -->
${true}           <!-- outputs: true -->
${false}          <!-- outputs: false -->
${[1, 2, 3]}      <!-- outputs: 1,2,3 -->
${[true, false]}  <!-- outputs: true,false -->
${['foo', 'bar']} <!-- outputs: foo,bar -->
${['foo', '']}    <!-- outputs: foo, -->
```

### Expression Options

Options modify expression behavior using `@ optionName` or `@ optionName=value` syntax:

```html
<!-- Option without value -->
${myVar @ optName}

<!-- Option with identifier or literal value -->
${myVar @ optName=myVar}
${myVar @ optName=true}
${myVar @ optName=42}
${myVar @ optName='string'}
${myVar @ optName="string"}
${myVar @ optName=[myVar, 'string']}

<!-- Multiple options -->
${myVar @ optOne, optTwo=myVar, optThree='string'}
```

## Expression Context and Escaping

### Context Types (Complete List)

| Context | When to use | What it does |
|---------|-------------|--------------|
| `text` | Default for content inside HTML Text Nodes | Encodes all HTML special characters |
| `html` | To safely output markup | Filters HTML to remove dangerous tags |
| `attribute` | Default for attribute values | Encodes all HTML special characters |
| `attributeName` | Default for data-sly-attribute when setting attribute names | Validates attribute name, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `elementName` | Default for data-sly-element | Validates element name, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `uri` | Default for `action`, `cite`, `data`, `formaction`, `href`, `manifest`, `poster`, `src` attributes | Validates URI, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `scriptToken` | For JavaScript identifiers, literal numbers, or literal strings | Validates JavaScript token, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `scriptString` | Within JavaScript strings | Encodes characters that would break out of string |
| `scriptComment` | Within JavaScript comments | Validates JavaScript comment, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `styleToken` | For CSS identifiers, numbers, dimensions, strings, hex colours or functions | Validates CSS token, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `styleString` | Within CSS strings | Encodes characters that would break out of string |
| `styleComment` | Within CSS comments | Validates CSS comment, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `number` | To display numbers | Validates value is a number, outputs nothing if validation fails |
| `unsafe` | When all other contexts are too restrictive | **Disables escaping and XSS protection completely** |

**Automatic Context Detection:**
- HTML attributes like `href`, `src`, `action` automatically use `uri` context
- Script and style contexts **require explicit context** - expression won't output if context isn't set

**Rules:**
- **Always specify context** for non-HTML output (JSON, attributes, scripts, styles)
- Use `@ context="unsafe"` only when content is already sanitized and verified safe
- Use `@ context="json"` when generating JSON in `.json.html` files
- Default context is `text` for content, `attribute` for attributes
- `elementName` context only allows specific HTML5 semantic elements (see spec)

### Format Option

#### String Formatting
```html
${'Asset {0}' @ format=properties.assetName}
${'Asset {0}' @ format=[properties.assetName]}
${'Asset {0} out of {1}' @ format=[properties.current, properties.total]}
${'Asset {0} out of {1}' @ format=[properties.current, properties.total], i18n, locale='de'}
```

#### Date Formatting
```html
${'yyyy-MM-dd' @ format=myDate}
${'yyyy-MM-dd' @ format=myDate, type='date'}
${'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm' @ format=myDate, timezone='GMT+00:30'}
${'EEEE, dd MMMM yyyy' @ format=obj.date, locale='de'}
```

**Date Pattern Letters:**
- `y` (yy, yyyy) - Year
- `M` (MM, MMM, MMMM) - Month
- `d` (dd) - Day in month
- `E` (EEEE) - Day name in week
- `H` (HH) - Hour in day (0-23)
- `h` (hh) - Hour in am/pm
- `m` (mm) - Minute
- `s` (ss) - Second
- `S` (SSS) - Millisecond
- `z`, `Z`, `X` - Time zones

#### Number Formatting
```html
${'#.00' @ format=42}
${'#.00' @ format=myNumber, type='number'}
${'#,###.00' @ format=1000}
${'#.###;-#.###' @ format=obj.number}
${'#%' @ format=obj.number}
```

**Number Pattern Characters:**
- `0` - digit, shows as 0 if absent
- `#` - digit, does not show if absent
- `.` - decimal separator
- `,` - grouping separator
- `;` - sub-pattern boundary (positive;negative)
- `%` - multiply by 100 and show as percentage
- `E` - separator between mantissa and exponent

### i18n Option
```html
${'Assets' @ i18n}                                    <!-- Translates to resource language -->
${'Assets' @ i18n, locale='en-US', hint='Translation Hint'}  <!-- Override locale with hint -->
```

**Rules:**
- Use for all user-facing strings
- `locale` overrides resource language (e.g., `en_US`, `fr_CH`)
- `hint` provides context for translators

### Array Join Option
```html
${['one', 'two'] @ join='; '}              <!-- outputs: one; two -->
<span class="${myListOfClassNames @ join=' '}"></span>
${'test' @ join=', '}                       <!-- outputs: test (no change for strings) -->
```

### URI Manipulation Options

```html
<!-- Scheme -->
${'//example.com/path' @ scheme='http'}     <!-- outputs: http://example.com/path -->
${'http://example.com' @ scheme='https'}     <!-- outputs: https://example.com -->

<!-- Domain -->
${'///path/page.html' @ domain='example.org'}  <!-- outputs: //example.org/path/page.html -->

<!-- Path manipulation -->
${'one' @ appendPath='two'}                  <!-- outputs: one/two -->
${'path' @ prependPath='..'}                 <!-- outputs: ../path -->
${'path/page.html' @ path='that/two'}        <!-- outputs: that/two.html -->

<!-- Selectors -->
${'path/page.woo.foo.html' @ selectors='foo.bar'}      <!-- outputs: path/page.foo.bar.html -->
${'path/page.woo.foo.html' @ addSelectors='foo.bar'}   <!-- outputs: path/page.woo.foo.foo.bar.html -->
${'path/page.woo.foo.html' @ removeSelectors='foo.bar'} <!-- outputs: path/page.woo.html -->

<!-- Extension -->
${'path/page' @ extension='html'}           <!-- outputs: path/page.html -->
${'path/page.json' @ extension='html'}       <!-- outputs: path/page.html -->

<!-- Suffix -->
${'path/page.html' @ suffix='my/suffix'}    <!-- outputs: path/page.html/my/suffix -->

<!-- Query -->
${'http://example.org/search' @ query=jsuse.query, context='uri'}
${'http://example.org/search?s=1' @ addQuery=jsuse.query, context='uri'}
${'http://example.org/search?s=1&q=htl' @ removeQuery='q', context='uri'}

<!-- Fragment -->
${'path/page' @ fragment='fragment'}         <!-- outputs: path/page#fragment -->
```

## JSON-HTL Templates (.json.html)

### Structure
```html
<sly data-sly-use.model="ai.typerefinery.websight.models.MyModel">
{
  "type": "${properties.type}",
  "props": {
    "name": "${properties.name}",
    "value": ${model.value @ context="json"},
    "items": [
      <sly data-sly-list.item="${model.items}">
        {
          "key": "${item.key}",
          "value": ${item.value @ context="json"}
        }<sly data-sly-test="${!itemList.last}">,</sly>
      </sly>
    ]
  }
}
</sly>
```

**Rules:**
- Use `@ context="json"` for all JSON values
- Handle comma separation manually with `itemList.last` checks
- Keep JSON structure valid (proper commas, brackets, quotes)
- Use `<sly>` tags for control flow, not HTML elements
- Boolean and numeric values don't need quotes in JSON

### Passing Configuration from .content.json to JS Components

**CRITICAL RULE**: When creating dialog components that render React/JS components, you MUST pass ALL configuration properties from `.content.json` to the JS component props, including optional flags.

**Pattern:**
```html
{
  "type": "/apps/typerefinery/components/dialog/select/Select.js",
  "props": {
    "name": "${properties.name}",
    "label": "${properties.label}",
    "description": "${properties.description}",
    "allowCreate": ${properties.allowCreate || false},
    "required": ${properties.required || false},
    "isColour": ${properties.isColour || false},
    "isIcon": ${properties.isIcon || false},
    "options": [...]
  }
}
```

**Rules:**
- **Always pass all properties**: Include ALL properties from `.content.json` that the JS component might use
- **Use fallback values**: Use `|| false` for booleans, `|| ''` for strings, `|| 0` for numbers
- **Consistent across branches**: If you have conditional rendering (e.g., datasource vs inline), pass the same props in ALL branches
- **Boolean handling**: Use `${properties.flag || false}` not `"${properties.flag}"` (no quotes for booleans in JSON)
- **Document props**: Document which properties are supported in component README

**Example with Conditional Branches:**
```html
<!--/* Datasource branch */-->
<sly data-sly-test="${datasourceChild}">
  {
    "type": "/apps/typerefinery/components/dialog/select/Select.js",
    "props": {
      "name": "${properties.name}",
      "label": "${properties.label}",
      "isColour": ${properties.isColour || false},
      "isIcon": ${properties.isIcon || false},
      "options": <sly data-sly-resource="${datasourceChild.path}"></sly>
    }
  }
</sly>
<!--/* Fallback branch - MUST include same props */-->
<sly data-sly-test="${!datasourceChild}">
  {
    "type": "/apps/typerefinery/components/dialog/select/Select.js",
    "props": {
      "name": "${properties.name}",
      "label": "${properties.label}",
      "isColour": ${properties.isColour || false},
      "isIcon": ${properties.isIcon || false},
      "options": [...]
    }
  }
</sly>
```

**Common Mistakes to Avoid:**
- ❌ Forgetting to pass optional flags in one branch but not the other
- ❌ Using quotes around boolean values: `"isColour": "${properties.isColour}"` (wrong)
- ❌ Missing fallback values: `"isColour": ${properties.isColour}` (will be `null` if undefined)
- ✅ Correct: `"isColour": ${properties.isColour || false}`

## Common Patterns

### Conditional Rendering with Fallback
```html
<!-- Find child resource by iterating (HTL doesn't support method calls with parameters) -->
<sly data-sly-list.child="${resource.children}">
  <sly data-sly-test="${child.name == 'datasource'}">
    <!-- Use datasource child -->
    <sly data-sly-use.datasource="${child}">
      <!-- Process datasource -->
    </sly>
  </sly>
</sly>
<!-- Fallback if no datasource found -->
<sly data-sly-test="${!datasource}">
  <!-- Fallback path -->
</sly>
```

### Resource Type Checking
```html
<sly data-sly-test="${item['sling:resourceType'] == 'my/component'}">
  <!-- Handle specific resource type -->
</sly>
```

### Null-Safe Property Access
```html
<!-- Use || operator for defaults -->
${properties.name || 'default'}

<!-- Check existence before access -->
<sly data-sly-test="${properties.name}">
  ${properties.name}
</sly>
```

### Array Operations
```html
<!-- Join array -->
${[item1, item2, item3] @ join=', '}

<!-- Format string -->
${'Hello {0}' @ format=model.name}
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Template Organization
- **Separate templates**: Put reusable templates in separate files
- **Template naming**: Use descriptive names: `item`, `list`, `card`, etc.
- **File structure**: Keep templates close to components that use them

### 2. Variable Naming
- **Model variables**: Use `model` for Sling Models
- **Template variables**: Use `template` for loaded templates
- **Item variables**: Use descriptive names: `item`, `child`, `paragraph`
- **Boolean flags**: Use descriptive names: `hasContent`, `isActive`, `isEmpty`

### 3. Performance
- **Minimize model calls**: Load models once, reuse variables
- **Avoid nested loops**: Flatten data structures in models when possible
- **Cache expensive operations**: Use `data-sly-set` for repeated calculations

### 4. Readability
- **Indentation**: Maintain consistent indentation (2 spaces)
- **Comments**: Add comments for complex logic
- **Line breaks**: Break long expressions across lines
- **Group related blocks**: Keep related HTL blocks together

### 5. Error Handling
- **Null checks**: Always check for null/empty before accessing properties
- **Default values**: Use `||` operator for defaults
- **Graceful degradation**: Provide fallback content when data is missing

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Don't: Inline Logic
```html
<!-- BAD: Complex logic in template -->
<sly data-sly-test="${model.items.size() > 0 && model.items.get(0).isActive}">
```

### ✅ Do: Logic in Model
```java
// In Sling Model
public boolean hasActiveItems() {
  return items != null && !items.isEmpty() && items.get(0).isActive();
}
```
```html
<!-- GOOD: Simple check in template -->
<sly data-sly-test="${model.hasActiveItems}">
```

### ❌ Don't: Missing Context
```html
<!-- BAD: Missing context for JSON -->
{
  "value": ${model.value}
}
```

### ✅ Do: Specify Context
```html
<!-- GOOD: Explicit context -->
{
  "value": ${model.value @ context="json"}
}
```

### ❌ Don't: Hardcoded Paths
```html
<!-- BAD: Hardcoded absolute path -->
<sly data-sly-use.template="/apps/myapp/components/template.html">
```

### ✅ Do: Relative Paths
```html
<!-- GOOD: Relative path -->
<sly data-sly-use.template="./template.html">
```

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] All expressions use appropriate context (`@ context="..."`)
- [ ] No business logic in templates (moved to Sling Models)
- [ ] Variables use descriptive names
- [ ] Null checks present for property access
- [ ] JSON templates use `@ context="json"` for values
- [ ] Comma separation handled correctly in JSON arrays
- [ ] Template calls use explicit parameter passing
- [ ] No hardcoded absolute paths
- [ ] Consistent indentation and formatting
- [ ] Comments added for complex logic

## Block Statement Priority

Block statements are evaluated in this order (highest to lowest priority):

1. `data-sly-include`
2. `data-sly-set`, `data-sly-test`, `data-sly-use`
3. `data-sly-call`
4. `data-sly-text`
5. `data-sly-element`, `data-sly-resource`
6. `data-sly-unwrap`
7. `data-sly-list`, `data-sly-repeat`
8. `data-sly-attribute`

When two block statements have the same priority, evaluation is left-to-right.

## Special HTML Tags

### `<sly>` Tag
```html
<!-- Remove element, show only children -->
<sly data-sly-resource="./header"></sly>

<!-- Display <sly> tag in output (not recommended) -->
<sly data-sly-unwrap="${false}"></sly>  <!-- outputs: <sly></sly> -->
```

**Rules:**
- `<sly>` is not a valid HTML5 tag but is supported by HTL
- Use to remove wrapper element, display only children
- Similar functionality to `data-sly-unwrap`
- Prefer `<sly>` over `data-sly-unwrap` for cleaner syntax

## Comments

### HTL Comments
```html
<!--/* HTL comments are removed from output */-->
<!--/* Can contain any characters except '*/-->' */-->
```

### HTML Comments with Expressions
```html
<!-- Page title: ${currentPage.jcr:title} -->
<!-- Expressions in HTML comments are evaluated, block statements are not -->
```

**Rules:**
- HTL comments: `<!--/* */-->` - completely removed from output
- HTML comments: `<!-- -->` - expressions evaluated, block statements ignored
- Use HTML comments for debugging output
- Use HTL comments for template documentation

## Whitespace

Whitespace is allowed between any part of an expression:

```html
<!-- Recommended spacing style -->
${myVar @ argOne, argTwo=myVar, argThree='string'}

<!-- Spaces everywhere (also valid) -->
${ myVar @ argOne , argTwo = myVar , argThree = 'string' }
```

**Rules:**
- Use consistent spacing style
- Recommended: no spaces around `@`, spaces after commas
- Spaces around `=` are optional but recommended for readability

## Clientlibs in HTL

### Including Clientlibs in HTL Templates

**REQUIRED**: Use `ClientLibsModel` Sling Model to include clientlibs (CSS and JS) in HTL templates.

**Pattern:**
```html
<sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
    categories='category-name',
    debug=true,
    crossorigin='anonymous'}">
  ${clientlibs.cssIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
  ${clientlibs.jsIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
</sly>
```

**Available Methods:**
- `cssIncludes`: Returns HTML `<link>` tags for CSS files
- `jsIncludes`: Returns HTML `<script>` tags for JS files
- `jsAndCssIncludes`: Returns both CSS and JS includes
- `cssInline`: Returns inline CSS content
- `jsInline`: Returns inline JS content

**Options:**
- `categories`: Required; comma-separated list of clientlib categories (e.g., `'ai.typerefinery.websight.components-vendor'`)
- `debug`: Optional boolean; adds debug comments and cache-busting query strings
- `crossorigin`: Optional string; sets `crossorigin` attribute (e.g., `'anonymous'`)
- `async`: Optional boolean; adds `async` attribute to script tags
- `defer`: Optional boolean; adds `defer` attribute to script tags
- `media`: Optional string; sets `media` attribute for CSS links
- `onload`: Optional string; sets `onload` attribute for script tags

**Rules:**
- **Always use `@ context="unsafe"`**: Clientlib includes return HTML strings that must be output unescaped
- **CSS in `<head>`**: Include CSS files in the page `<head>` section
- **JS in `<body>`**: Include JS files at the end of `<body>` or in appropriate sections
- **Category naming**: Use consistent category names following the pattern `ai.typerefinery.websight.components-*`
- **Debug mode**: Use `debug=true` during development to see which clientlibs are loaded

**Example: Including CSS in Head**
```html
<head>
  <!-- Vendor CSS (Bootstrap, Font Awesome, etc.) -->
  <sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
      categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components-vendor',
      debug=true,
      crossorigin='anonymous'}">
    ${clientlibs.cssIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
  </sly>
  
  <!-- Component-specific CSS -->
  <sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
      categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components',
      debug=true}">
    ${clientlibs.cssIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
  </sly>
</head>
```

**Example: Including JS in Body**
```html
<body>
  <!-- Page content -->
  
  <!-- Vendor JS -->
  <sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
      categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components-vendor',
      debug=true,
      crossorigin='anonymous'}">
    ${clientlibs.jsIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
  </sly>
  
  <!-- Component-specific JS -->
  <sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
      categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components',
      debug=true}">
    ${clientlibs.jsIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
  </sly>
</body>
```

**Example: Including Both CSS and JS**
```html
<sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
    categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components-editor',
    debug=true}">
  ${clientlibs.jsAndCssIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
</sly>
```

### Standard Clientlib Categories

**Vendor Libraries** (`ai.typerefinery.websight.components-vendor`):
- Bootstrap CSS/JS
- Font Awesome CSS
- jQuery
- Other third-party libraries
- **Location**: `/apps/typerefinery/components/clientlibs/clientlibs-vendor/`

**Header Libraries** (`ai.typerefinery.websight.components-header`):
- Libraries needed in page header
- **Location**: `/apps/typerefinery/components/clientlibs/clientlibs-header/`

**Footer Libraries** (`ai.typerefinery.websight.components-footer`):
- Libraries loaded at end of body
- **Location**: `/apps/typerefinery/components/clientlibs/clientlibs-footer/`

**Component Libraries** (`ai.typerefinery.websight.components`):
- Component-specific CSS/JS
- Auto-discovered from component `clientlibs/` folders
- **Location**: `/apps/typerefinery/components/*/clientlibs/`

**Editor Libraries** (`ai.typerefinery.websight.components-editor`):
- Editor-specific libraries
- **Location**: `/apps/typerefinery/components/clientlibs/clientlibs-editor/`

### Creating Clientlib Definitions

**Structure:**
```
/apps/typerefinery/components/clientlibs/my-clientlib/
├── .content.json
└── README.md (optional)
```

**`.content.json` format:**
```json
{
  "title": "My Client Library",
  "jcr:primaryType": "sling:Folder",
  "sling:resourceType": "io/typerefinery/websight/clientlibs",
  "categories": ["ai.typerefinery.websight.components-mycategory"],
  "css": [
    "/apps/typerefinery/clientlibs/my-lib/style.css"
  ],
  "js": [
    "/apps/typerefinery/clientlibs/my-lib/script.js"
  ]
}
```

**Rules:**
- Use `sling:resourceType`: `"io/typerefinery/websight/clientlibs"`
- Define `categories` array with at least one category
- Use absolute paths starting with `/apps/` for CSS and JS files
- Paths should point to actual files in the repository

### Page Structure Integration

**Standard page structure includes clientlibs automatically:**

1. **`head.html`** includes `head-libs.html`:
   ```html
   <head>
     <!-- ... meta tags ... -->
     <sly data-sly-include="head-libs.html"></sly>
   </head>
   ```

2. **`head-libs.html`** includes vendor, header, and component CSS:
   ```html
   <!-- Vendor CSS -->
   <sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
       categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components-vendor',
       debug=true,
       crossorigin='anonymous'}">
     ${clientlibs.cssIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
   </sly>
   <!-- ... other categories ... -->
   ```

3. **`body.html`** includes `body-end.html`:
   ```html
   <body>
     <sly data-sly-include="body-content.html"></sly>
     <sly data-sly-include="body-end.html"></sly>
   </body>
   ```

4. **`body-end.html`** includes vendor, header, footer, and component JS:
   ```html
   <!-- Vendor JS -->
   <sly data-sly-use.clientlibs="${'ai.typerefinery.websight.clientlibs.ClientLibsModel' @
       categories='ai.typerefinery.websight.components-vendor',
       debug=true,
       crossorigin='anonymous'}">
     ${clientlibs.jsIncludes @ context="unsafe"}
   </sly>
   <!-- ... other categories ... -->
   ```

**Rules:**
- Pages using the standard page structure automatically get clientlibs
- Custom pages must manually include clientlibs using the pattern above
- Always include CSS in `<head>` and JS at end of `<body>`

### Component-Specific Clientlibs

**Pattern**: Component clientlibs are auto-discovered if placed in:
```
/apps/typerefinery/components/my-component/
└── clientlibs/
    ├── .content.json
    ├── style.css
    └── script.js
```

**`.content.json` format:**
```json
{
  "sling:resourceType": "io/typerefinery/websight/clientlibs",
  "categories": ["ai.typerefinery.websight.components"],
  "css": [
    "/apps/typerefinery/components/my-component/clientlibs/style.css"
  ],
  "js": [
    "/apps/typerefinery/components/my-component/clientlibs/script.js"
  ]
}
```

**Rules:**
- Use category `ai.typerefinery.websight.components` for auto-discovery
- Component clientlibs are automatically included when the page includes component category
- Keep component clientlibs scoped to the component to avoid conflicts

### Debugging Clientlibs

**Enable debug mode:**
- Add `debug=true` to `ClientLibsModel` options
- Or add `?debug=true` to page URL query string
- Debug mode adds HTML comments showing which clientlibs are loaded

**Verify clientlibs are loaded:**
```javascript
// Check if CSS is loaded
const cssLinks = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('link[rel="stylesheet"]'));
console.log('CSS files:', cssLinks.map(l => l.href));

// Check if JS is loaded
const jsScripts = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('script[src]'));
console.log('JS files:', jsScripts.map(s => s.src));
```

**Common Issues:**
- **Clientlibs not loading**: Check category name matches exactly
- **CSS not applying**: Verify CSS file path is correct and file exists
- **JS errors**: Check JS file path and browser console for errors
- **Missing dependencies**: Ensure vendor libraries load before component libraries

